{
    "title": "Providing for the Closing of Executive Departments and Agencies of the Federal Government on December 24, 2025, and December 26, 2025",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "December 23, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-23/pdf/2025-23847.pdf",
    "summary": "The federal government will close executive departments and agencies on Wednesday, December 24, 2025, and Friday, December 26, 2025. These days bookend Christmas and will be treated under the usual federal pay and leave rules, while agency leaders can still require some staff to work if needed for national security, defense, or other public needs.\n\nFor many federal employees, this means extra time to rest, travel, and be with family without losing pay, which can boost morale and work-life balance during a busy season. Families of federal workers benefit too, and local businesses near federal offices could see holiday traffic shift to earlier in the week. Essential services\u2014such as public safety, national defense, and critical operations\u2014will remain staffed where necessary, helping ensure continuity for matters that cannot pause.\n\nOn the other hand, people who planned to visit federal offices or rely on certain services those days may face delays. Tasks like in-person appointments, document processing, and customer support could be pushed to the following week, adding to year-end backlogs. Some federal employees in essential roles may still have to report to work, which can be disappointing when others are off. Contractors may experience mixed effects depending on their employer and contract terms, since this closure does not automatically extend the same pay and leave provisions to them.\n\nIn short, this move offers a meaningful break for most federal workers while keeping critical functions running. Anyone with time-sensitive needs involving a federal agency should plan around the closures on December 24 and 26.",
